# Line endings. The repo is LF throughout and `.pre-commit-config.yaml` already
# enforces that with `mixed-line-ending --fix=lf` — but that hook only runs on
# files a contributor is committing, *after* checkout has already decided what
# landed in the working tree. Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`,
# so without this file a Windows checkout (a contributor's, or the CI runner's)
# materializes every text file as CRLF, and the hook then rewrites them into a
# diff nobody asked for. `text=auto eol=lf` makes the working tree LF on every
# platform, which is also what the Windows CI legs test against.
#
# `text=auto` leaves Git's own binary detection in charge, so images and fonts
# are untouched; the explicit `binary` lines below are belt-and-braces for the
# formats where a stray "looks like text" heuristic would corrupt the file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored fonts and print-resolution guide figures — byte-for-byte upstream.
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary

# C64 payloads: SID tunes, PRGs, cartridge images, raw memory dumps. These
# carry $0d/$0a bytes as data, not as line terminators.
*.sid binary
*.prg binary
*.crt binary
*.d64 binary
*.bin binary
